  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  A few strong thunderstorms are possible this afternoon and\n   evening. \n\n-  Significant rain event for much of the Northern Rockies Sunday\n   through Tuesday morning.\n\n-  Gradual drying trend develops mid-week alongside morning \n   valley fog.\n\n-  72% of ensemble clusters depict another weather-maker to \n   impact the Northern Rockies next weekend. \n\nVery impressive thunderstorms occurred overnight across western\nMontana! Besides the lightning show, these were heavy rain\nproducers with reports of close to 0.40\" of rain falling in only \n15 minutes in Florence in the Bitterroot Valley. The rainfall has\nfallen in a narrow swath from Lemhi County northwards across\nMissoula County then northeast towards Powell County and beyond.\n\nThis activity was in response to shortwaves that have been\nembedded in the southerly flow, connected with the large upper\nlevel low over Washington and Oregon State. There could be a brief\nbreak in the showers later this morning into the early afternoon\nbefore another round of storms will develop. The latest\nconvection-allowing models depict a few stronger storms developing\nover the Bitteroot Valley, Granite County, even extending\nnorthwards towards Flathead County this afternoon and evening. The\nlatest HRRR model is showing that a few of these storms could\nproduce one inch hail. Also with the atmospheric moisture content\nbeing 150% of normal for this time of year, the storms will be\ncapable of bringing very heavy rain rates on the order of 0.50\" to\n1\" per hour. The heavy rain in particular could cause localized\nflooding issues, particularly if a flood prone area or burn scar\ntakes a direct hit. \n\nThe upper low continues to be on track to move from near Boise,\nIdaho tonight to near central Montana by Monday afternoon. This \nwill set the stage for widespread moderate to heavy rainfall \nacross western Montana. NOAA's Weather Prediction Center has \nplaced a \"slight\" risk for excessive rainfall for northwest \nMontana including Glacier National Park on Monday. This means that\nthere is at least a 15% risk of rainfall exceeding flash flood \nguidance within that area. Much of the northwest Montana area\ncould see 0.10 to 0.20\" per hour rainrates, but locally higher\nrates are possible. Locations that receive the higher end totals \nmay see rapid rises on small streams and creek, rock and debris \nslides onto roads, and minor flooding in poor drainage areas.\nTotal rain amounts will range between 1 and 3 inches across\nFlathead County, and some mountain ranges like the Mission, Swan\nand Lewis Ranges have a 30% probability in exceeding 3 inches.  \n\nShowers will persist on Tuesday mainly across northwest Montana\nwhile other areas will start to moderate. Lingering boundary-layer\nmoisture will support overnight and early morning fog and low\nstratus clouds on Wednesday and possibly Thursday.  \n\nTemperatures will rebound Wednesday through Friday, getting back\nto near seasonal levels for this time of year...70s and 80s for\nthe lower elevations of Idaho. \n\nFor the longer range, including next weekend, a lot hinges on what\nevolves well upstream of us! One of the deepest troughs so far \nthis season across eastern Russia will support a very amplified \nridge over Alaska later this week. This will in turn support a \ndeepening trough over western Canada. This kind of setup can lead\nto another trough digging over the Pacific Northwest which could\nbring unsettled and cooler weather for us. Some of the guidance\nare depicting a colder trough scenario which could bring lower\nsnow levels. In fact, the NBM is starting to show 20 to 30% chance\nfor snow above 7,000 feet along the Divide next Saturday!",
